"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,610\nThe Learning Brain. Series 8: Assessment That Teaches\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,336\nEpisode 6\n\n3\n00:00:07,416 --> 00:00:09,807\nSpecifications grading\n\n4\n00:00:12,607 --> 00:00:15,256\nWhat if, instead of points and partial credit,\n\n5\n00:00:15,570 --> 00:00:18,554\nevery assignment were marked either meets the standard,\n\n6\n00:00:18,858 --> 00:00:21,566\nor not yet, with a chance to revise?\n\n7\n00:00:21,922 --> 00:00:23,877\nThat is specifications grading.\n\n8\n00:00:24,047 --> 00:00:27,598\nLinda Nilson described the approach in 2014.\n\n9\n00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:32,010\nEach task has clear specifications tied to course outcomes.\n\n10\n00:00:32,407 --> 00:00:34,609\nWork either meets them or does not.\n\n11\n00:00:35,108 --> 00:00:37,884\nCourse grades come from how many outcomes,\n\n12\n00:00:37,915 --> 00:00:39,250\nor bundles of tasks,\n\n13\n00:00:39,427 --> 00:00:43,018\nstudents complete, often with tokens for revisions.\n\n14\n00:00:43,188 --> 00:00:45,787\nAdvocates claim it reduces anxiety,\n\n15\n00:00:46,071 --> 00:00:50,752\nclarifies expectations, motivates students to master the material,\n\n16\n00:00:50,974 --> 00:00:52,679\nand saves grading time.\n\n17\n00:00:52,849 --> 00:00:54,270\nWhat does the evidence show?\n\n18\n00:00:54,762 --> 00:01:01,714\nCarnegie Mellon's Eberly Center summarized it this way: research is concentrated in science and engineering,\n\n19\n00:01:01,947 --> 00:01:05,852\nvaries in rigor, and yields inconsistent findings.\n\n20\n00:01:06,234 --> 00:01:09,532\nMany claims rest on experience and student surveys.\n\n21\n00:01:09,702 --> 00:01:14,157\nTheir own study, from 2023 to 2025,\n\n22\n00:01:14,483 --> 00:01:20,635\ncompared nine instructors' courses taught with traditional points and then with specifications grading.\n\n23\n00:01:21,212 --> 00:01:24,477\nThe picture was neither an endorsement nor a rejection.\n\n24\n00:01:24,938 --> 00:01:27,395\nEffects depended on how it was implemented,\n\n25\n00:01:27,637 --> 00:01:28,382\nand where.\n\n26\n00:01:28,552 --> 00:01:33,917\nStudent surveys do often report lower anxiety and clearer expectations.\n\n27\n00:01:34,368 --> 00:01:37,012\nThat fits older research by Ruth Butler,\n\n28\n00:01:37,372 --> 00:01:42,697\nwhich found that comments focused on the task supported interest better than grades alone.\n\n29\n00:01:42,867 --> 00:01:43,839\nSome caution.\n\n30\n00:01:44,239 --> 00:01:50,008\nSpecs grading can create its own stress if the bar is unclear or revisions are scarce,\n\n31\n00:01:50,235 --> 00:01:52,392\nand it can add work for instructors.\n\n32\n00:01:52,753 --> 00:01:54,564\nThe design details matter.\n\n33\n00:01:54,734 --> 00:01:55,520\nFor teaching.\n\n34\n00:01:55,895 --> 00:01:57,725\nIf you try it,\n\n35\n00:01:57,922 --> 00:01:59,490\nstart small: one assignment type,\n\n36\n00:01:59,510 --> 00:02:01,962\nwith clear written specifications and examples.\n\n37\n00:02:02,273 --> 00:02:04,046\nAllow at least one revision.\n\n38\n00:02:04,437 --> 00:02:07,491\nTell students exactly how the course grade is built.\n\n39\n00:02:07,903 --> 00:02:10,993\nAnd check what happens to learning,\n\n40\n00:02:11,421 --> 00:02:13,267\nnot just satisfaction.\n\n41\n00:02:13,499 --> 00:02:16,112\nOne tip to share with students: treat not yet as a guide to revise.\n\n42\n00:02:16,282 --> 00:02:19,575\nClear standards and a second chance are good ideas.\n\n43\n00:02:19,924 --> 00:02:22,317\nHow you build them decides whether they work.\n\n"